  "notes": "West leads the ♥K against 3♠. The defense plays 3 rounds of hearts. South must ruff the third round of hearts small. As East has played the ♥A and ♥J, West must have all of the remaining 15 high card points for his 1NT opening.\n\nWest must have the guarded ♦K giving South a certain diamond loser. South must, therefore, avoid losing 2 trump tricks. West is known to have the ♠K and ♠J making this task more difficult. There are three trump positions that South can play for.\n\nFirst, West could have doubleton ♠KJ. In that case, South can play trumps for 1 loser by ducking the first or second round of trumps completely. Second, West could have 4 spades missing the ♠10. In that case, South can hold his trump losers to 1 by playing the ♠Q through West.\n\nThe best play in the trump suit, however, is known as an intra-finesse. This play will succeed when West has 3 spades including the ♠K, the ♠J, and either the ♠5 or ♠4. Since there are two cases in which the intra-finesse works, it is superior to the other two plays. The other two plays each cater only to one case.\n\nSouth should cross to the ♣K and lead a low spade. If East plays the ♠10, South covers and West wins the ♠K. South can later finesse against the ♠J. If East instead follows low, South plays low and West wins the ♠J. South can later lead the ♠Q, picking up West's ♠K and pinning East's ♠10.\n\nAfter drawing trumps South can concede a trick to the West's ♦K.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♥K against 3♠. The defense plays 3 rounds of hearts. South must ruff the third round of hearts small. As East has played the ♥A and ♥J, West must have all of the remaining 15 high card points for his 1NT opening.",
    "West must have the guarded ♦K giving South a certain diamond loser. South must, therefore, avoid losing 2 trump tricks. West is known to have the ♠K and ♠J making this task more difficult. There are three trump positions that South can play for.",
    "First, West could have doubleton ♠KJ. In that case, South can play trumps for 1 loser by ducking the first or second round of trumps completely. Second, West could have 4 spades missing the ♠10. In that case, South can hold his trump losers to 1 by playing the ♠Q through West.",
    "The best play in the trump suit, however, is known as an intra-finesse. This play will succeed when West has 3 spades including the ♠K, the ♠J, and either the ♠5 or ♠4. Since there are two cases in which the intra-finesse works, it is superior to the other two plays. The other two plays each cater only to one case.",
    "South should cross to the ♣K and lead a low spade. If East plays the ♠10, South covers and West wins the ♠K. South can later finesse against the ♠J. If East instead follows low, South plays low and West wins the ♠J. South can later lead the ♠Q, picking up West's ♠K and pinning East's ♠10.",
    "After drawing trumps South can concede a trick to the West's ♦K."
